1
00:00:00,768 --> 00:00:07,267
You're paying roughly two hundred and fifty thousand dollars a year for an internal-tools platform.

2
00:00:07,287 --> 00:00:09,833
Our recommendation: don't replace it.

3
00:00:09,859 --> 00:00:14,600
Keep Power Apps where it earns its keep, and pilot Devin-assisted custom development

4
00:00:14,627 --> 00:00:16,647
on the next engineering-owned tool.

5
00:00:17,177 --> 00:00:21,527
A working prototype makes the case, and shows exactly where the boundaries are.

6
00:00:23,264 --> 00:00:26,900
First, be clear about what that license actually buys.

7
00:00:26,947 --> 00:00:29,607
Screens are the visible part.

8
00:00:30,365 --> 00:00:36,655
The real value is the platform underneath: Microsoft Entra identity and single sign-on.

9
00:00:36,695 --> 00:00:41,633
Dataverse, a managed data layer with row and column level security.

10
00:00:41,675 --> 00:00:44,500
More than a thousand governed connectors.

11
00:00:44,539 --> 00:00:46,500
Data loss prevention policies.

12
00:00:46,535 --> 00:00:50,895
And managed lifecycle controls, all maintained by Microsoft.

13
00:00:51,909 --> 00:00:54,833
Custom code inherits none of that for free.

14
00:00:54,860 --> 00:01:01,140
Any honest build-versus-buy comparison starts there, not with how fast you can render a table.

15
00:01:02,606 --> 00:01:07,000
To test the build side, Devin built a working operations console.

16
00:01:07,017 --> 00:01:13,287
One deep KYC review workflow, plus refunds and feature flags as thinner reuse tests.

17
00:01:13,937 --> 00:01:19,700
Underneath: one server-side permission map, declarative state machines, and an append-only

18
00:01:19,720 --> 00:01:24,727
audit event written in the same database transaction as every business change.

19
00:01:25,016 --> 00:01:28,729
A record cannot change without an audit trail, by construction.

20
00:01:29,551 --> 00:01:33,131
All data is synthetic, and identity is deliberately simulated.

21
00:01:33,131 --> 00:01:36,411
The point is the pattern, not a production system.

22
00:01:37,861 --> 00:01:39,593
Here's the real application.

23
00:01:39,733 --> 00:01:45,967
A reviewer works the KYC queue, opens a case, assigns it to themselves, and approves

24
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,073
it with a mandatory decision note.

25
00:01:48,758 --> 00:01:55,100
The case moves through a controlled state machine, from pending to in review to approved,

26
00:01:55,133 --> 00:01:59,753
and once a decision is final, the server refuses any further transition.

27
00:01:59,746 --> 00:02:06,473
Every action lands in the case's audit timeline: who, what, before and after.

28
00:02:07,288 --> 00:02:13,533
Ten automated tests pin these boundaries down, covering permitted actions, denied actions,

29
00:02:13,553 --> 00:02:18,553
invalid transitions, and audit atomicity, against the real service code.

30
00:02:19,968 --> 00:02:22,960
The same spine repeats across modules.

31
00:02:22,960 --> 00:02:27,920
Refunds reuse the same queue, dialog, and audit components.

32
00:02:28,287 --> 00:02:33,733
Feature flags show the permission boundary: as a reviewer, the toggle is refused, enforced

33
00:02:33,767 --> 00:02:36,720
on the server, not just hidden in the interface.

34
00:02:37,351 --> 00:02:42,300
Switch to an admin, and enabling a production flag requires a recorded change reason.

35
00:02:42,340 --> 00:02:47,920
And every action, across every module, shows up in one central audit log.

36
00:02:49,467 --> 00:02:53,047
Devin's role is the economics of the second through the tenth app.

37
00:02:53,067 --> 00:02:59,500
The repository encodes the conventions: a machine-readable rulebook and a module playbook,

38
00:02:59,534 --> 00:03:03,087
so the next workflow follows the same golden path.

39
00:03:03,626 --> 00:03:09,967
Schema, service, permissions, transitions, audit, tests, pull request.

40
00:03:10,010 --> 00:03:12,732
Reviewed and merged by your engineers.

41
00:03:13,903 --> 00:03:16,892
Devin is an accelerator inside your delivery process.

42
00:03:16,899 --> 00:03:23,532
It is not the runtime, not the identity provider, and not the accountable owner of what ships.

43
00:03:24,932 --> 00:03:30,333
And what the prototype deliberately does not replicate: enterprise single sign-on, the

44
00:03:30,363 --> 00:03:37,467
connector ecosystem, a managed data platform, centralized data loss prevention, citizen

45
00:03:37,494 --> 00:03:45,127
development, and production operations, meaning monitoring, incident response, and disaster recovery.

46
00:03:45,974 --> 00:03:50,167
On the custom path, your team owns security, compliance, and support.

47
00:03:50,665 --> 00:03:55,700
Those are real, recurring costs, and they gate any migration decision, especially for

48
00:03:55,717 --> 00:03:57,849
regulated workflows like KYC.

49
00:03:59,407 --> 00:04:02,200
So route by app, not by ideology.

50
00:04:02,239 --> 00:04:06,787
Keep the existing three apps where they are; there's no migration case yet.

51
00:04:07,340 --> 00:04:12,400
Build custom only when engineering will own the tool in production, it needs code-level

52
00:04:12,437 --> 00:04:17,933
control or deep internal integration, and the workflow is well-defined enough to test.

53
00:04:17,974 --> 00:04:20,387
Everything else stays on Power Apps.

54
00:04:21,137 --> 00:04:26,533
Concretely: confirm the real seat mix behind the spend, take one pilot, feature-flag

55
00:04:26,580 --> 00:04:32,547
administration, to a true production bar with real identity and monitoring, and measure it.

56
00:04:33,155 --> 00:04:34,733
Don't replace the platform.

57
00:04:34,751 --> 00:04:37,347
Earn the right to shrink it, with evidence.
